.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_FULL 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_full \- declare and create an ordered map with custom allocation and comparison
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_full(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs,
                     custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle named hm_name with the companion type
variables that carry key_t and val_t to every later type-inferred macro,
then creates a map that takes its memory from mmgmt_procs and orders its
keys with custom_comparison_proc.

This is the widest of the construct macros. cbmap_construct(3) is the
same thing with the default allocator and the default order,
cbmap_construct_mp(3) passes only an allocator and cbmap_construct_cc(3)
only a comparison procedure. Either argument can be NULL to select the
matching default, so this one macro covers every combination.

If the creation fails the macro stops the program through
_ccol_fatal_err(). When the caller has to handle a failure without it
being fatal, declare the handle first and use cbmap_init_full(3).
cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3) is the same macro with a cleanup attribute
that destroys the map when the scope exits.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                   Key type. Every later macro reads a key through
                        it, and the container takes from it the width to
                        expect and the alignment of a stored key
val_t                   Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read
                        it; the library never passes it to the container
mmgmt_procs             Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the
                        default allocator
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro declares variables and doesn't expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure receives pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value
when the first key orders before the second, a positive value when the
first orders after the second, and zero when the map must treat them as
the same key. A zero makes the call an update rather than an insert, so
the procedure defines key equality together with key order.

The order it gives must be a strict weak order over key_t that doesn't
change while the map holds elements: one pair of keys always compares the
same way, the reverse comparison gives the opposite sign, and the order is
transitive. An inconsistent procedure breaks the placement invariant of
the tree, after which the map can't find a key that it holds.

The procedure gets two bare pointers and no lengths, so it can only read a
width that key_t fixes. cbmap_create_full(3) describes the default orders
that a procedure replaces, and the order that a struct key type gets when
there is no procedure.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
If the caller passes a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, none of its four members may
be NULL. The map copies the procedures into itself, but the procedures and
the comparison procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map.

Every tree node of the map is allocated through these procedures, and
everything the map frees goes back through them.

A walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order that the
map was given; cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract.

Destroy the map with cbmap_destroy(3), or use
cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3) to have the exit of the scope destroy it.

The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle, so a function
that receives the handle needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there.

An array key type or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], gives a
compile error whose message says to wrap the array in a struct. The macros
copy keys and values by assignment, C cannot assign an array, and the map
would otherwise store the address of the caller's array rather than its
elements. A struct holding the array works.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};
int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b);

cbmap_construct_full(releases, version, char *, &procs, version_cmp);
cbmap_destroy(releases);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_construct (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_mp (3),
